<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taghaza</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taghaza was a salt-mining centre in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, in the north of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>. Its rock-salt deposits were among the most important sources of salt for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, exchanged southwards for gold. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a> describe how salt from the Taghaza mines moved through Songhai territory as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-trade">trans-Saharan gold</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-trade">salt trade</a> that enriched the imperial treasury.</p></div>
